WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.754 --> 00:00:01.792
- [Narrator] Smell.

00:00:01.792 --> 00:00:05.499
We know when it's good,
and we know when it's bad,

00:00:05.499 --> 00:00:07.328
but there's a woman in Scotland -

00:00:07.328 --> 00:00:08.868
- [Voiceover] I'm Joy Milne.

00:00:08.868 --> 00:00:11.237
- [Narrator] Who can
smell the unsmellable.

00:00:11.237 --> 00:00:13.245
- [Voiceover] My nose went on fire.

00:00:13.245 --> 00:00:15.676
- [Narrator] Joy Milne
is the only known person

00:00:15.676 --> 00:00:19.034
who can smell Parkinson's Disease.

00:00:19.034 --> 00:00:22.592
- [Voiceover] I have an extremely
sensitive sense of smell.

00:00:22.592 --> 00:00:24.019
(sniffing)

00:00:24.019 --> 00:00:27.149
I can smell it mainly on the forehead

00:00:27.149 --> 00:00:28.591
and the back of the neck.

00:00:28.591 --> 00:00:31.702
- [Narrator] To Joy, it
has a greasy musky odor.

00:00:31.702 --> 00:00:34.198
- [Voiceover] It is just
a heavy smell to me.

00:00:34.198 --> 00:00:36.332
- [Narrator] She first noticed
it on her late husband,

00:00:36.332 --> 00:00:39.966
Les Milne twelve years
before he was diagnosed.

00:00:39.966 --> 00:00:41.546
- [Voiceover] I just thought he was tired

00:00:41.546 --> 00:00:43.395
and he wasn't showering enough.

00:00:43.395 --> 00:00:44.654
It wasn't very welcome of course,

00:00:44.654 --> 00:00:47.074
me saying to him, well, you're smelling.

00:00:47.074 --> 00:00:49.114
- [Narrator] It wasn't until
she attended a conference

00:00:49.114 --> 00:00:51.480
with other Parkinson's patients that

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.146
she realized the smell was something more.

00:00:54.146 --> 00:00:57.302
- [Voiceover] When I walked
into the room I thought gosh,

00:00:57.302 --> 00:01:00.438
these other people smell
the same as Les does.

00:01:00.438 --> 00:01:02.421
At the end of the meeting
I stood up and said,

00:01:02.421 --> 00:01:05.930
why were they not investigating
the smell of Parkinson's.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:07.430
- [Narrator] No one was investigating

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:09.658
because no one else could smell it.

00:01:09.658 --> 00:01:11.746
Researchers set up a controlled experiment

00:01:11.746 --> 00:01:13.510
to test Joy's nose.

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:15.485
She was asked to distinguish
a set of t-shirts

00:01:15.485 --> 00:01:18.792
worn by those with and
without the disease.

00:01:18.792 --> 00:01:20.334
She insisted one of the subjects

00:01:20.334 --> 00:01:23.121
in the control group had the musky scent.

00:01:23.121 --> 00:01:25.437
- [Voiceover] The man rang
about eight months later

00:01:25.437 --> 00:01:27.421
and said, “I’ve got Parkinson’s.”

00:01:27.421 --> 00:01:30.060
So, I had pre-diagnosed somebody.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.135
- [Narrator] By the time
most people are diagnosed

00:01:32.135 --> 00:01:35.342
80 to 90 percent of the damage is done.

00:01:35.342 --> 00:01:37.360
So Joy's mission is to help scientists

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:40.422
improve early detection methods.

00:01:40.422 --> 00:01:43.026
- [Voiceover] If they can
find these people earlier,

00:01:43.026 --> 00:01:45.624
it would stop this anguish.

00:01:45.624 --> 00:01:48.457
That would make such a difference.

